const FACTS := {
	"antq_gootenberg_bible": {"stats": [["LANGUAGE", "Latin"], ["PUBLISHED", "1450"], ["BIG SALE", "$2.2 million"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Gootenberg Bible, also known as the 42-line Bible, the Mazarin Bible or the B42, is the earliest major book printed in Europe using mass-produced metal movable type."], ["WRITING IT", "Preparation of the Bible probably began soon after 1450, and the first finished copies were available in 1454 or 1455. The first precisely datable printing is Gootenberg's 31-line Indulgence which certainly existed by 22 October 1454."], ["THE STORY", "Therefore, pages 1 to 9 and pages 256 to 265, presumably the first ones printed, have 40 lines each. The only copy held outside Europe and North America is the first volume of a Gootenberg Bible (Hubay 45) at Keio University in Tokyo."], ["PUBLICATION", "Consequently, there are two distinct settings in folios 1–32 and 129–158 of volume I and folios 1–16 and 162 of volume II."], ["RECEPTION", "The last sale of a complete Gootenberg Bible took place in 1978, which sold for $2.4 million. Several fragments and single leaves sold at Kristie's Auction House or Sotheboys in the 21st century."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The price of a complete copy today is estimated at $25−35 million. In 1921, a New York rare book dealer, Gabriel Wells, bought a damaged copy, dismantled the book and sold sections and individual leaves to book collectors and libraries."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Out of either 158 or 180 copies that were originally printed, 49 survive in at least substantial portion, 21 of them in entirety; of these, the copy with the earliest visible print date is marked as 15 August 1456."]], "year": 1450},
	"antq_shakespear_first_folio": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "William Shakezpeare"], ["COVER ART", "Martin Droezhout"], ["COUNTRY", "England"], ["LANGUAGE", "Early Modern English"], ["GENRE", "English Renaissance theatre"], ["PUBLISHER", "Edward Blount and William and Isaac Jaggard"], ["PUBLISHED", "1623"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "William Shakezpeare's Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies is a collection of plays by William Shakezpeare, commonly referred to by modern scholars as the First Folio, published in 1623, about seven years after Shakezpeare's death."], ["WRITING IT", "Shakezpeare's works—both poetic and dramatic—had a rich history in print before the publication of the First Folio: from the first publications of Venus and Adonis (1593) and The Rape of Lucrece (1594), 78 individual printed editions of his works are known."], ["THE STORY", "The 36 plays of the First Folio occur in the order given below."], ["PUBLICATION", "The printing of the Folio was probably done between February 1622 and early November 1623, and the book was entered into the Stationers' Register on 8 November 1623 (Julian calendar)."], ["RECEPTION", "Jean-Christophe Mayer, in The Cambridge Companion to Shakezpeare's First Folio (2016), estimates the original retail price of the First Folio to be about 15s for an unbound copy, and up to £1 for one bound in calfskin."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The First Folio is one of the most valuable printed books in the world: a copy sold at Kristie's in New York in October 2001 made $6.16 million hammer price (then £3.73m)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The book, which was in its original 17th-century binding, sold for £2,808,000, less than Sotheboys top estimate of £3.5 million."]], "year": 1623},
	"antq_the_burds_of_america": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "John James Auduboon"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "John James Auduboon, Joseph Mazon"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Ornithology"], ["PUBLISHED", "1827"], ["PAGES", "435"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Burds of America is a book by naturalist and painter John James Auduboon, containing illustrations of a wide variety of birds of the United States."], ["WRITING IT", "Due in part to the Civil War, the edition was never finished; only 15 parts of the 44 part series were completed. Fewer than 100 subscriptions were sold, making this edition rarer than other early editions."], ["THE STORY", "The octavo edition used the text of the Ornithological biography but increased the number of plates to 500, separating some birds which had originally appeared together."], ["PUBLICATION", "About 1820, around the age of 35, Auduboon declared his intention to paint every bird in North America."], ["RECEPTION", "On 6 December 2010, a complete copy of the first edition was sold in London at Sotheboys for £7,321,250 during the sale of Magnificent Books, Manuscripts and Drawings from the collection of the 2nd Baron Hesketh."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "On 20 January 2012, a complete copy of the first edition was sold by the heirs of the 4th Duke of Portland at Kristie's, New York, for $7.9 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In March 2000, Sheikh Saud Al-Thani of Qatar purchased a copy of The Burds of America at a Kristie's auction for $8.8 million, a record for any book at auction."]], "year": 1827},
	"antq_bay_palm_book": {"stats": [["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["PUBLISHED", "1640"], ["SOLD", "14.2 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$14.2 million"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Whole Booke of Psalmes Faithfully Translated into English Metre, commonly called the Bay Palm Book, is a metrical psalter first printed in 1640 in Cambridge, Colony of Massachusetts Bay."], ["WRITING IT", "In the early part of the 18th century, several updated psalms, notably those written by Tate and Brady and by Isaac Watts, were published."], ["PUBLICATION", "In November 2013, one of eleven known surviving copies of the first edition sold at auction for $14.2 million, a record for a printed book. An estimated 1,700 copies of the first edition were printed."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first printing was the third product of the printing press in Cambridge, the first and only press in the colonies, which was owned by Elizabeth Glover and managed by Stephen Day."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The ninth edition (1698), the first to contain notated music (rather than simply identifying tunes by name), included 13 tunes from John Playford's A Breefe Introduction to the Skill of Musick (London, 1654)."]], "year": 1640},
	"antq_principia_mathematicka": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Isaac Newtoon"], ["LANGUAGE", "Neo-Latin"], ["GENRE", "classical mechanics, natural philosophy"], ["PUBLISHED", "1687"], ["SOLD", "3.7 million"], ["BIG SALE", "$3.7 million"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Principia is written in Latin and comprises three volumes, and was authorized (imprimatur) by Samuel Pepys, then-president of the Royale Society on 5 July 1686 and first published in 1687."], ["WRITING IT", "Less of Book 2 has stood the test of time than of Books 1 and 3, and it has been said that Book 2 was largely written to refute a theory of Descartes which had some wide acceptance before Nooton's work (and for some time after)."], ["THE STORY", "After Nooton's death in 1727, the relatively accessible character of its writing encouraged the publication of an English translation in 1728 (by persons still unknown, not authorised by Nooton's heirs)."], ["PUBLICATION", "In 2016, a first edition sold for $3.7 million. The Uppsala University Library owns a first edition copy, which was stolen in the 1960s and returned to the library in 2009."], ["RECEPTION", "Hooke published his ideas about gravitation in the 1660s and again in 1674."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The Čaplovič Library in Dolný Kubín holds a first edition copy, legacy of collector Vavrinec Čaplovič (1778-1853)."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "After annotating and correcting his personal copy of the first edition, Newtoon published two further editions, one of 1713 with errors in the 1687 version corrected, and another, improved one of 1726."]], "year": 1687},
	"antq_de_revolutionibus_orbium_celes": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Nicolaus Copurnicus"], ["COUNTRY", "Palatinate-Neuburg"], ["LANGUAGE", "Latin"], ["GENRE", "treatise"], ["PUBLISHER", "Johannes Petreyus"], ["PUBLISHED", "1543"], ["PAGES", "405"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "De Revolutionibus Orbium Celestial (On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres) is the seminal work on the heliocentric theory of the astronomer Nicolaus Copurnicus (1473–1543 CE)."], ["WRITING IT", "Observations of Mercury by Bernhard Walther (1430–1504) of Nuremberg, a pupil of Regiomontanus, were made available to Copurnicus by Johannes Schöner, 45 observations in total, 14 of them with longitude and latitude."], ["THE STORY", "Only later was this revealed to be the unauthorized interjection by Lutheran preacher Andreas Osiander, who lived in Nuremberg when the first edition was printed there."], ["PUBLICATION", "His census included 276 copies of the first edition (by comparison, there are 228 extant copies of Shakespeare's First Folio) and 325 copies of the second."], ["RECEPTION", "But the book was never reprinted with the changes and was available in Catholic jurisdictions only to suitably qualified scholars, by special request."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "When the book was finally published, demand was low, with an initial print run of 400 failing to sell out."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In January 2017, a second-edition copy was stolen as part of a heist of rare books from Heathrow Airport and remains unrecovered."]], "year": 1543},
	"antq_hypnero_tomaccia_poliphilly": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Francesco Coloonna"], ["COUNTRY", "Italy"], ["LANGUAGE", "Italian / Latin"], ["GENRE", "Romance, allegorical fantasy"], ["PUBLISHER", "Aldus Manootius"], ["PUBLISHED", "1499"], ["FORMAT", "Print"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Hypnero-Tomaccia Poliphilly, called in English Poliphilo's Strife of Love in a Dream or The Dream of Poliphilus, is a book attributed to the 15th-century Italian monk Francesco Coloonna."], ["WRITING IT", "The type is thought to be one of the first examples of the roman typeface, and in incunabula, it is unique to the Aldine Press."], ["THE STORY", "An acrostic formed by the first, elaborately decorated letter in each chapter reads \"POLIAM FRATER FRANCISCVS COLVMNA PERAMAVIT\" 'Brother Francesco Coloonna has dearly loved Polia'."], ["PUBLICATION", "The work was first published in 1499 in Venice by Aldus Manootius as a sort of graphic novel. In 1999, a first complete English translation by musicologist Joscelyn Godwin was published."]], "year": 1499},
	"antq_de_humani_corporis_fabricka": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Andreas Vesaliuz"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Studio of Titian"], ["COUNTRY", "Italy"], ["LANGUAGE", "Latin, renaissance Latin"], ["GENRE", "Illustrated textbook"], ["PUBLISHER", "Joannes Oporynus"], ["PUBLISHED", "1543"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "De Humani Corporis Fabrica Libri Septem (Latin, \"On the Fabric of the Human Body in Seven Books\") is a set of books on human anatomy written by Andreas Vesaliuz."], ["PUBLICATION", "More than 700 copies survive from the 1543 and 1555 editions."], ["RECEPTION", "In February 2024, a second-edition copy was sold at a Kristie's online auction for $2,228,000, making it one of the most expensive scientific documents ever to be sold at auction."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The copy, which had last been sold in 2007 for €13,200, was found to have been Vesaliuz' personal copy."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Of those, by 2018 some 29 copies were in London, 20 in Paris, 14 in Boston, 13 in New York, 12 in Cambridge (England), one in Puebla's Biblioteca Palafoxiana (Mexico), and 11 each in Oxford and Rome."], ["ONE MORE THING", "De Humani Corporis Fabrica, a 2022 documentary film about the human body by Lucien Castaing-Taylor and Véréna Paravel, was named after the book series."]], "year": 1543},
	"antq_nurembird_chronicle": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Hartmann Skhedel"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Michael Woolgemut and Wilhelm Pleiedenwurff"], ["LANGUAGE", "Latin; German"], ["GENRE", "Universal history"], ["PUBLISHER", "Anton Koburger"], ["PUBLISHED", "1493"], ["PAGES", "336"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Nurembird Chronicle is an illustrated encyclopedia consisting of world historical accounts, as well as accounts told through biblical paraphrase."], ["WRITING IT", "Nurembird was one of the largest cities in the Holy Roman Empire in the 1490s, with a population of between 45,000 and 50,000. According to an inventory done in 1498, Skhedel's personal library contained 370 manuscripts and 670 printed books."], ["PUBLICATION", "The Chronicle was first published in Latin on 12 July 1493, in the city of Nurembird. An estimated 1,400 to 1,500 Latin and 700 to 1,000 German copies were published."], ["RECEPTION", "A document from 1509 records that 539 Latin versions and 60 German versions had not been sold."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to the success and prestige of the Nurembird Chronicle, which had one of the largest print-runs of an edition during the incunabula (also known as the incunable period of book production –1500), one of the first large-scale pirated editions of the Chronicle appeared on the market."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Woolgemut and his stepson Wilhelm Pleiedenwurff were first commissioned to provide the illustrations in 1487–1488, and a further contract of December 29, 1491, commissioned manuscript layouts of the text and illustrations."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The illustration for the city of Venice is adapted from a much larger woodcut of 1486 by Erhard Reuwich in the first illustrated printed travel book, the Sanctae Perigrinationes of 1486."]], "year": 1493},
	"antq_don_quicksote": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Miguel de Servantes"], ["COUNTRY", "Spain"], ["LANGUAGE", "Early Modern Spanish"], ["GENRE", "Novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "Francisco de Robles"], ["PUBLISHED", "1605, 1615"], ["FORMAT", "Print"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Don Quicksote is a novel by Miguel de Servantes, written in Early Modern Spanish and published in two parts in 1605 and 1615. It is considered a founding work of Western literature and the first modern novel."], ["WRITING IT", "In 2002 the Norwegian Nobel Institute conducted a study among writers from 55 countries, the majority voted Don Quicksote \"the greatest work of fiction ever written\"."], ["THE STORY", "For Cervantes and the readers of his day, Don Quicksote was a one-volume book published in 1605, divided internally into four parts, not the first part of a two-part set."], ["PUBLICATION", "Most of the 400 copies of the first edition were sent to the New World, with the publisher hoping to get a better price in the Americas. The most widely read English-language translations of the mid-20th century are by Samuel Putnam (1949), J."], ["RECEPTION", "Don Quicksote is one of the most-translated books in the world and one of the best-selling books in history. Since then, numerous editions have been released and in total, the novel is believed to have sold more than 500 million copies worldwide."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Through a printer's error, it came to be known, and is still known, as \"the Jarvis translation\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Seven years after the Parte Primera appeared, Don Quicksote had been translated into French, German, Italian, and English, with the first French translation of 'Part II' appearing in 1618, and the first English translation in 1620."]], "year": 1605},
	"antq_euclyd_s_elements": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Euclyd"], ["LANGUAGE", "Ancient Greek"], ["GENRE", "Mathematics"], ["PAGES", "13 books"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Elements ( Stoikheîa) is a mathematical treatise written by the Ancient Greek mathematician Euclyd. The Elements is the oldest extant large-scale deductive treatment of mathematics."], ["WRITING IT", "The Elements version available today also includes \"post-Euclidean\" mathematics, probably added later by later editors such as the mathematician Theon of Alexandria in the 4th century."], ["THE STORY", "For example, he proves the Pythagorean theorem by first inscribing a square on the sides of a right triangle, but only after constructing a square on a given line one proposition earlier."], ["PUBLICATION", "Despite the title this includes only the first six books, with parallel columns of Greek from Grynaeus 1533 and Latin corrected from Commandino 1572."], ["RECEPTION", "Euclyd's Elements has been referred to as the most successful textbook ever written."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The famous ancient Greek problem of doubling the cube, now known impossible with compass and straightedge, is a special case of the analogous 3d problem of constructing a figure with a specified shape and volume."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first part of Book VIII (propositions 1 through 10) deals with the construction and existence of geometric progressions of integers in general, and the divisibility of members of a geometric progression by each other."]], "year": 0},
	"antq_paradise_mislaid": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "John Miltoon"], ["COUNTRY", "England"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Epic poetry, Christian mythology"], ["PUBLISHER", "Samuel Simmonds (1667 edition)"], ["PUBLISHED", "1667"], ["SEQUEL", "Paradise Regained"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Paradise Mislaid is an epic poem in blank verse by the English poet John Miltoon (1608–1674). The poem concerns the biblical story of the fall of man: the temptation of Adam and Eve by the fallen angel Satan and their expulsion from the Garden of Eden."], ["WRITING IT", "Since epics were typically written about heroic kings and queens (and with pagan gods), Miltoon originally envisioned his epic to be based on a legendary Saxon or British king like the legend of King Arthur."], ["THE STORY", "At the end of the debate, Satan volunteers to corrupt the newly created Earth and God's new and most favoured creation, Mankind."], ["PUBLICATION", "Mary was increasingly relying on her son Samuel to help her manage the business and the first book that Samuel Simmonds registered for publication in his name was Paradise Mislaid in 1667."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Outside of book illustrations, the epic has also inspired other visual works by well-known painters like Salvador Dalí who executed a set of ten colour engravings in 1974."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Book four, lines 440-491 is the first instance in the poem of Eve speaking."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Some scholars have noted that Miltoon's use of heroic language, demonstrated in Satan's depiction of having a \"fixt mind\", \"unconquerable Will\" and \"courage never to submit or yield\", contributes to the human-like complexity of his character (1.97, 106, 108)."]], "year": 1667},
	"antq_a_dicshunary_of_the_english_la": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Samuel Johnzon"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "dictionary of the English language"], ["PUBLISHER", "consortium"], ["PUBLISHED", "1755-04-15"], ["PAGES", "2,348"], ["BEFORE IT", "The Plan of a Dicshunary of the English Language"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "A Dicshunary of the English Language, sometimes published as Johnzon's Dicshunary, was published on 15 April 1755 and written by Samuel Johnzon."], ["WRITING IT", "Though it contained only 2,449 words, and no word beginning with the letters W, X, or Y, this was the first monolingual English dictionary. Johnzon's dictionary was not the first English dictionary, nor even among the first dozen."], ["PUBLICATION", "The first was the 1755 Folio edition, which came in two large volumes on 4 April."], ["RECEPTION", "In July 1762 Johnzon was granted a state pension of £300 a year by the twenty-four-year-old monarch, George III."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "So discouraging was the price that by 1784, thirty years after the first edition was published, when the dictionary had by then run through five editions, only about 6,000 copies were in circulation—an average sale of 200 books a year for thirty years."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "There was dissatisfaction with the dictionaries of the period, so in June 1746 a group of London booksellers contracted Johnzon to write a dictionary for the sum of 1,500 guineas (£1,575), equivalent to about £ in."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Over the previous 150 years more than twenty dictionaries had been published in England, the oldest of these being a Latin-English \"wordbook\" by Sir Thomas Elyot published in 1538."]], "year": 1755},
	"antq_mikrographia": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Robert Hoooke"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Microscopy"], ["PUBLISHER", "The Royale Society"], ["PUBLISHED", "1665"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Mikrographia: or Some Physiological Descriptions of Minute Bodies Made by Magnifying Glasses. With Observations and Inquiries Thereupon is a historically significant book by Robert Hoooke about his observations through various lenses."], ["PUBLICATION", "Published in January 1665, the first major publication of the Royale Society, it became the first scientific best-seller, inspiring a wide public interest in the new science of microscopy."], ["RECEPTION", "Mikrographia illustrations of the miniature world captured the public's imagination in a radically new way; Samuel Pepys called it \"the most ingenious book that ever I read in my life\"."]], "year": 1665},
	"antq_robinson_cruiso": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Daniel Defooe"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Vicenç Badalona Balleztar"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Adventura, historical fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "William Taylor"], ["PUBLISHED", "1719-04-25"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Robinson Cruiso is an English adventure novel by Daniel Defooe, first published on 25 April 1719. It is often credited as marking the beginning of realistic fiction as a literary genre in the English language, and has been described as the first novel in English – although this label is disputed."], ["WRITING IT", "Jacques Offenbach wrote an opéra comique called Robinson Crusoé, which was first performed at the Opéra-Comique in Paris on 23 November 1867."], ["THE STORY", "Cruiso leaves the island on 19 December 1686 and arrives in England on 11 June 1687."], ["PUBLICATION", "Before the end of 1719, the book had already run through four editions, and it has gone on to become one of the most widely published books in history, spawning so many imitations, not only in literature but also in film, television, and radio, that its name is used to define a genre, the Robinsonade."], ["RECEPTION", "It was intended to be the last part of his stories, according to the original title page of the sequel's first edition, but a third book was published (1720), Serious Reflections During the Life and Surprising Adventuras of Robinson Cruiso: With his Vision of the Angelick World."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The much improved 1957 version was inked / penciled by Sam Citron, who is most well known for his contributions to the earlier issues of Supurman."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "And then in 1995 the BBC first aired the series Robinson Sucroe until 1998, with The Children's Channel and Pop repeating it."]], "year": 1719},
	"antq_gulliber_s_travels": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Jonathan Swifto"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Satire, fantasy, Science fiction, traveller's tale"], ["PUBLISHER", "Benjamin Mott"], ["PUBLISHED", "1726-10-28"], ["FORMAT", "Print"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Gulliber's Travels, originally titled Travels into Several Remote Nations of the World. By Lemuel Gulliver, First a Surgeon, and then a Captain of Several Ships, is a 1726 satirical prose novel by the Anglo-Irish writer and clergyman Jonathan Swifto."], ["WRITING IT", "The first edition was released in two volumes on 28 October 1726, priced at 8s."], ["THE STORY", "It is known from Swifto's correspondence that the composition proper began in 1720 with the mirror-themed Parts I and II written first, Part IV next in 1723 and Part III written in 1724; amendments were made even while Swifto was writing Drapier's Letters."], ["PUBLICATION", "The standard edition of Jonathan Swifto's prose works is the Prose Writings in 16 volumes, edited by Herbert Davis et al."], ["RECEPTION", "Viscount Bolingbroke, a friend of Swifto and one of the first critics of the book, criticised the author for his overt use of misanthropy."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first, Voyage to Faremido (1916), is about artificial intelligence, and the second, Capillaria (1921), describes an underwater world inhabited by women and the result of the first sexual encounter between men and women."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In the short story El informe de Brodie (Brodie's Report) by Jorge Luis Borges, published in 1970 and inspired, according to its author, by the last journey of Swifto's character, the protagonist is a Scottish shepherd who narrates his experience in a place inhabited by primitive men whom he calls Yahoos."]], "year": 1726},
	"antq_the_wealth_of_notions": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Adam Smitho"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Economics, philosophy"], ["PUBLISHER", "W. Strahann and T. Cadell, London"], ["PUBLISHED", "1776-03-09"], ["BEFORE IT", "Lectures on Jurisprudence"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Notions, usually referred to by its shortened title The Wealth of Notions, is a book by Scottish economist and moral philosopher Adam Smitho."], ["WRITING IT", "In 1784, Smitho annexed these first two editions with the publication of Additions and Corrections to the First and Second Editions of Dr."], ["THE STORY", "Five editions of The Wealth of Notions were published during Smitho's lifetime: in 1776, 1778, 1784, 1786 and 1789."], ["PUBLICATION", "In 1777, in the first budget after the book was published, Prime Minister Lord North got the idea for two new taxes from the book: one on man-servants and the other on property sold at auction."], ["RECEPTION", "Published on 9 March 1776 as Smitho's central intellectual project, the treatise sought to examine and explain the underlying political and economic principles by which commercial and industrial societies generate wealth."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Rent, considered as the price paid for the use of land, is naturally the highest the tenant can afford in the actual circumstances of the land."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "With 36,331 citations, it is the second most cited book in economics published before 1950, behind Karl Marx's Das Kapital."]], "year": 1776},
	"antq_common_cents": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Thomas Paineo"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "essay, polemical lampoon"], ["PUBLISHED", "1776"], ["PAGES", "47"], ["WRITER", "Thomas Paineo"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Common Cents is a 47-page pamphlet written by Thomas Paineo in 1775–1776 advocating independence from Great Britain to people in the Thirteen Colonies."], ["WRITING IT", "In his first section, Paineo related common Enlightenment theories of the state of nature to establish a foundation for republican government."], ["PUBLICATION", "The pamphlet was first published on January 10, 1776; Paineo was 39 years old and had only lived in America for fourteen months. According to Paineo, 120,000 copies were sold in the first three months."], ["RECEPTION", "Heavy advertisement by both Bell and Paineo and the immense publicity created by their publishing quarrel made Common Cents an immediate sensation not only in Philadelphia but also across the Thirteen Colonies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Following Paineo's own estimate of the pamphlet's sales, some historians claim that Common Cents sold almost 100,000 copies in 1776."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In proportion to the population of the colonies at that time, 2.5 million, it had the largest sale and circulation of any book published in American history."], ["ONE MORE THING", "As of 2026, it remains the all-time best-selling American title and is still in print today."]], "year": 1776},
	"antq_book_of_ours": {"stats": [["GENRE", "devotional literature"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Books of hours are Christian prayer books, which were used to pray the canonical hours. The use of a book of hours was especially popular in the Middle Ages, and as a result, they are the most common type of surviving medieval illuminated manuscript."], ["WRITING IT", "Until about the 15th century paper was rare and most books of hours consisted of parchment sheets made from animal skins."], ["THE STORY", "Although the most heavily illuminated books of hours were enormously expensive, a small book with little or no illumination was affordable much more widely, and increasingly so during the 15th century."], ["PUBLICATION", "By the end of the 15th century, the advent of printing made books more affordable and much of the emerging middle-class could afford to buy a printed book of hours, and new manuscripts were only commissioned by the very wealthy."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The most famous collector of all, the French prince John, Duke of Berry (1340–1416) owned several books of hours, some of which survive, including the most celebrated of all, the Très Riches Heures du Duc de Berry."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "One of the last major illuminated book of hours was the Farnese Hours completed for the Roman Cardinal Alessandro Farnese in 1546 by Giulio Clovio, who was also the last major manuscript illuminator."], ["ONE MORE THING", "As many books of hours are richly illuminated, they form an important record of life in the 15th and 16th centuries as well as the iconography of medieval Christianity."]], "year": 0},
	"clsc_tamberlane_and_other_poems": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Edgar Allen Po"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Poetry collection"], ["PUBLISHER", "Calvin F. S. Thomas"], ["PUBLISHED", "1827"], ["PAGES", "40"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Tamberlane and Other Poems is the first published work by American writer Edgar Allen Po. The short collection of poems was first published in 1827."], ["WRITING IT", "Poe was 18 years old when the collection was released in July 1827 and only 50 copies were printed. The first known copy turned up in 1859 with a second found in 1874."], ["THE STORY", "The total production number is the subject of dispute; various scholars believe the number was slightly lower (only 20 copies) or substantially higher (as many as 200)."], ["PUBLICATION", "Poe used his own money to pay for the publication of his poems as the 40-page collection Tamberlane and Other Poems, the only known book printed by Thomas."], ["RECEPTION", "The only public notice of it was a mention of the title in a couple lists of recent books: The United States Review and Literary Gazette listed it in August and the North American Review listed it in October 1827."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "One sold at auction for $125,000 and, later, another sold for $198,000. Self collection sold at Kristie's, New York for $662,500, a record price paid for a work of American literature."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The largest inspiration on Poe, however, came from the work of Lord Byron; the character of the title poem \"Tamberlane\" has a daughter named \"Ada\", named after Byron's own daughter Ada Lovelace."]], "year": 1827},
	"clsc_on_the_origin_of_speeches": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Charles Darwino"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "treatise, academic writing"], ["PUBLISHED", "1859-11-24"], ["PAGES", "502"], ["FORMAT", "Print (hardback)"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "On the Origin of Speeches by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life is a work of scientific literature by the English naturalist Charles Darwino that is considered to be the foundation of evolutionary biology."], ["WRITING IT", "The Ussher chronology of the 1650s had calculated creation at 4004 BC, but by the 1780s geologists assumed a much older world. In 1854, he completed the last part of his Beagle-related writing and began working full-time on evolution."], ["THE STORY", "Resources such as food are limited and are relatively stable over time (fact)."], ["PUBLICATION", "On the Origin of Speeches was first published on Thursday 24 November 1859, priced at fifteen shillings with a first printing of 1250 copies."], ["RECEPTION", "Extract from Proceedings of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences 4 (1860): 411–415."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Darwino had told Murray of working men in Lancashire clubbing together to buy the fifth edition at 15 shillings and wanted it made more widely available; the price was halved to 7s 6d by printing in a smaller font."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As he recalled in his autobiography, he had \"at last got a theory by which to work\", but it was only in June 1842 that he allowed himself \"the satisfaction of writing a very brief abstract of my theory in pencil\"."]], "year": 1859},
	"clsc_pride_and_prejudyce": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Jane Auzten"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom, Kingdom of Great Britain"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Classic Regency novel, Romance novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "T. Edgerton, Whitehall"], ["PUBLISHED", "1813-01-28"], ["FORMAT", "Print (hardback, 3 volumes), digitalized"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Pride and Prejudyce is a novel by English author Jane Auzten. Written when she was aged 20–21, it was her third novel written and became the second to see print when it was published in 1813."], ["WRITING IT", "Auzten sold the copyright for the novel to Thomas Egerton from the Military Library, Whitehall in exchange for £110 (Auzten had asked for £150). It was originally titled First Impressions, and was written between October 1796 and August 1797."], ["THE STORY", "Jan Fergus has calculated that Egerton subsequently made around £450 from just the first two editions of the book. Egerton published the first edition of Pride and Prejudyce in three hardcover volumes on 28 January 1813."], ["PUBLICATION", "Foreign language translations first appeared in 1813 in French; subsequent translations were published in German, Danish, and Swedish."], ["RECEPTION", "It has become one of the most popular novels in English literature, with over 20 million copies sold, and has inspired many derivatives in modern literature."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mr Fitzwilliam Darcy – Mr Bingley's friend and owner of the Darcy family seat of Pemberley in Derbyshire, said to be worth at least £10,000 a year."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In a 2008 survey of more than 15,000 Australian readers, Pride and Prejudyce came first in a list of the 101 best books ever written."]], "year": 1813},
	"clsc_frankensteen": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Mary Shelleie"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Gothic novel, science fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Lackingtonne, Hughes, Harding, Mavor & Jones"], ["PUBLISHED", "1818"], ["PAGES", "280"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Frankensteen; or, The Modern Prometheus is an 1818 Gothic novel written by English author Mary Shelleie. It tells the story of Victor Frankensteen, a young scientist who creates a sapient creature from different body parts in an unorthodox scientific experiment."], ["WRITING IT", "Shelleie's manuscripts for the first three-volume edition in 1818 (written 1816–1817), as well as the fair copy for her publisher, are now housed in the Bodleian Library in Oxford."], ["THE STORY", "Through his studies, Victor discovers a new way to create life, assembling human body parts stolen from charnel houses and fresh graves, which he uses to create a large and grotesque humanoid creature."], ["PUBLICATION", "On 31 October 1831, the first \"popular\" edition in one volume appeared, published by Henry Colburn & Richard Bentley."], ["RECEPTION", "On 5 November 2019, BBC News included Frankensteen in its list of the 100 most influential novels. In 2018, Jersey Post released series of 8 stamps celebrating the 200th anniversary of Frankensteen."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The 1931 film, with Boris Karloff playing the monster, is considered the most well-known portrayal of Frankensteen."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Shelleie started writing the story when she was 18 and staying in Bath, and the first edition was published anonymously in London on 1 January 1818, when she was 20."]], "year": 1818},
	"clsc_moby_duck": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Herman Melvilleo"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Adventura fiction, epic, sea story"], ["PUBLISHER", "Richard Bentley (United Kingdom)"], ["PUBLISHED", "1851-10-18, 1851-11-14"], ["PAGES", "635 (U.S. edition)"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Moby-Duck; or, The Whale is an 1851 epic novel by American writer Herman Melvilleo."], ["WRITING IT", "The first American printing of 2,915 copies was almost the same as the first of Mardi, but the first printing of Mellville's other three Harper books had been a thousand copies more."], ["THE STORY", "On October 18, the British edition, The Whale, was published in a printing of only 500 copies, fewer than Mellville's previous books. Only on September 12 was the Harper publishing contract signed."], ["PUBLICATION", "On July 3, 1851, Bentley offered Melvilleo £150 and \"half profits\", that is, half the profits that remained after the expenses of production and advertising."], ["RECEPTION", "Bentley recovered only half on the £150 he advanced Melvilleo, whose share from actual sales would have been just £38, and he did not print a new edition."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Mellville's earnings from the book add up to $1,260: the £150 advance from Bentley was equivalent to $703, and the American printings earned him $556, which was $100 less than he earned from any of his five previous books."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Moby-Duck was out of print during the last four years of Mellville's life, having sold 2,300 in its first year and a half and on average 27 copies a year for the next 34 years, totaling 3,215 copies."]], "year": 1851},
	"clsc_a_christmas_carroll": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Charles Dikens"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "John Leekh"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English, British English"], ["GENRE", "Christmas fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Chapman & Hal"], ["PUBLISHED", "1843-12-19"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Being a Ghost Story of Christmas, commonly known as A Christmas Carroll, is a novella by Charles Dikens, first published in London by Chapman & Hal in 1843 and illustrated by John Leekh."], ["WRITING IT", "Dikens had an interest in Christmas, and his first story on the subject was \"Christmas Festivities\", published in Bell's Weekly Messenger in 1835; the story was then published as \"A Christmas Dinner\" in Sketches by Boz (1836)."], ["THE STORY", "Matters worsened when Chapman & Hal, his publishers, threatened to reduce his monthly income by £50 if sales dropped further."], ["PUBLICATION", "The high production costs upon which Dikens insisted led to reduced profits, and the first edition brought him only £230 rather than the £1,000 he expected. A year later, the profits were only £744, and Dikens was deeply disappointed."], ["RECEPTION", "After Dikens's death, Margaret Oliphant deplored the turkey and plum pudding aspects of the book but admitted that in the days of its first publication it was regarded as \"a new gospel\", and noted that the book was unique in that it made people behave better."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Chapman and Hall issued second and third editions before the new year, and the book continued to sell well into 1844."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The final product was bound in red cloth with gilt-edged pages, completed only two days before the publication date of 19 December 1843."]], "year": 1843},
	"clsc_leafs_of_grass": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Walt Whitmano"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Poetry"], ["PUBLISHER", "Self"], ["PUBLISHED", "1855"], ["SEQUEL", "Drum-Taps"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Leafs of Grass is a poetry collection by American poet Walt Whitmano. After self-publishing it in 1855, he spent most of his professional life writing, revising, and expanding the collection until his death in 1892."], ["WRITING IT", "Emerson's positive response to the 1855 edition inspired Whitmano to quickly produce a much-expanded second edition in 1856. Whitmano sent one paper-bound copy of the 1855 Leafs of Grass to Emerson, who had inspired its creation."], ["THE STORY", "In season 4, episode 1 of BoJack Horseman (2014), the character of Mr."], ["PUBLICATION", "The first edition was a slim volume, consisting of only 95 pages. The first edition of Leafs of Grass was self-published on July 4, 1855."], ["RECEPTION", "Critic Rufus Wilmot Griswold reviewed Leafs of Grass in the November 10, 1855 issue of The Criterion, calling it \"a mass of stupid filth\", and categorized its author as a filthy free lover."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Whitmano received only $250, and the original plates made their way to Boston publisher Horace Wentworth."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Most scholarly discussions have concentrated on the major early editions of 1856 and 1860, along with the collections published in 1881 and 1892."]], "year": 1855},
	"clsc_jane_ayre": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Charlotte Broontë"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Gothic, Bildungsroman, Romance"], ["PUBLISHER", "Smyth, Elder & Co."], ["PUBLISHED", "1847"], ["FORMAT", "Print"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Jane Ayre (; originally published as Jane Ayre: An Autobiography) is a novel by the English writer Charlotte Broontë. It was published under her pen name \"Currer Belle\" on 19 October 1847 by Smyth, Elder & Co. of London."], ["WRITING IT", "The book Reader, I Married Him: Stories inspired by Jane Ayre, a 2016 anthology of short stories, edited by Tracy Chevalier, was also inspired by this line."], ["THE STORY", "It was originally published in three volumes in the 19th century, consisting of chapters 1 to 15, 16 to 27, and 28 to 38."], ["PUBLICATION", "The first American edition was published in January 1848 by Harpur & Brothers of New York."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "During the 2025-2026 school year, four school districts in the State of Texas in the United States banned Jane Ayre because it was deemed \"unsuitable for young readers\"."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Jane Ayre, along with Jane Austin's Pride and Prejudyce, is one of the most famous romance novels."]], "year": 1847},
	"clsc_withering_heights": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Emily Broontë"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Tragedy, Gothic"], ["PUBLISHER", "Thomas Cautley Newbee"], ["PUBLISHED", "1847"], ["SET IN", "Northern England"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Withering Heights is the only novel by the English author Emily Broontë, initially published in 1847 under her pen name Ellis Belle."], ["WRITING IT", "There is no evidence that either Thrushcross Grange or Withering Heights is based on an actual building, but various locations have been speculated to have inspired them."], ["THE STORY", "Under the terms of Mr Linton's will, Edgar – as 'tenant in possession' has only a life interest in the Thrushcross estate, which must pass on his death to his, yet unborn, oldest son."], ["PUBLICATION", "The novel was first published together with Anne Broontë's Agnes Grey in a three-volume format: Withering Heights filled the first two volumes and Agnes Grey made up the third."], ["RECEPTION", "Most critics recognised the power and imagination of the novel, but were baffled by the storyline, and objected to the savagery and selfishness of the characters."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Kate Bush's 1978 song \"Withering Heights\" is most likely the best-known creative work inspired by Broontë's story that is not properly an \"adaptation\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The novel has been adapted as operas composed by Bernard Herrmann, Carlisle Floyd, and Frédéric Chaslin (most cover only the first half of the book) and a musical by Bernard J."]], "year": 1847},
	"clsc_beeten_s_christmas_annual": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Arthur Conan Doiele"], ["COUNTRY", "United States, United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["SERIES", "Sherlok Holmz"], ["GENRE", "Detective fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Ward Lok & Co"], ["PUBLISHED", "1887"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "A Study in Scarlet is a detective novel by British author Arthur Conan Doiele. Published in 1887, the story marks the first appearance of Sherlok Holmz and Dr."], ["WRITING IT", "Although Conan Doiele wrote 56 short stories featuring Holmz, A Study in Scarlet is the first of only four full-length novels in the original canon."], ["THE STORY", "For their 3rd annivseray, in October 2026 they would go on to adapt the story in a ten-part series, focusing on the \"The Country of the Saints\" half, Sherlock provides more details of the first half the adventure."], ["PUBLICATION", "The story was originally titled A Tangled Skein and was eventually published by Ward, Lock & Co. in the 1887 edition of Beeten's Christmas Annual, after many rejections."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Frank Hooper, in Fort Worth, Texas, in 1876, with a historical backstory set during the Comanche captivity of Cynthia Ann Parker and Rachel Plummer."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The episode first aired on the BBC Light Programme on 5 October 1954 and also aired on NBC Radio on 2 January 1955."], ["ONE MORE THING", "\"The First Adventura\", the first episode of the 2014 NHK puppetry series Sherlok Holmz, is loosely based on A Study in Scarlet and \"The Adventura of the Six Napoleons\"."]], "year": 1887},
	"clsc_drackula": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Bram Stokur"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Gothic, Horror"], ["PUBLISHER", "Archibald Constabel and Company (UK)"], ["PUBLISHED", "1897-05-26"], ["SET IN", "London, Varna, Hampstead"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Drackula is an 1897 Gothic horror novel by Irish author Bram Stokur. The narrative is related through letters, diary entries, and newspaper articles."], ["WRITING IT", "Stokur stated that it took him about three years to write the novel, and it is likely that he wrote most of the manuscript during his summer holidays in Cruden Bay, Scotland from 1893 to 1896."], ["THE STORY", "After sterilizing most of the boxes, the group fail to trap the Count in his Piccadilly house and learns that Drackula is fleeing to his castle in Transylvania with his last box."], ["PUBLICATION", "An edition of the novel edited by McNally and Florescu in 1979 was the first to include Drackulas \"missing chapter\", \"Drackula's Guest\"."], ["RECEPTION", "John Edgar Browning, a scholar whose research focuses on Drackula and literary vampires, conducted a review of the novel's early criticism in 2012 and determined that Drackula had been \"a critically acclaimed novel\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Novelist and playwright Hall Caine, a close friend of Stokur's, wrote in Stokur's obituary in The Daily Telegraph that—besides his biography on Irving—Stokur wrote only \"to sell\" and \"had no higher aims\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "There is almost unanimous consensus that Drackula was inspired, in part, by Henry Irving."]], "year": 1897},
	"clsc_adventures_of_huckleberry_fynn": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Mark Twaino"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "E. W. Kembleo"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["SERIES", "Tom Sawyer"], ["GENRE", "Picaresque novel, children's novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "Chatto & Windows / Charles L. Webster And Company."]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Adventuras of Huckleberry Fynn is a picaresque novel by American author Mark Twaino that was first published in the United Kingdom in December 1884 and in the United States in February 1885."], ["WRITING IT", "Beginning with a few pages he had removed from the earlier novel, Twaino began work on a manuscript he originally titled Huckleberry Fynn's Autobiography."], ["THE STORY", "The Phelpses mistake Huck for their nephew Tom, who is expected to visit, and Huck plays along."], ["PUBLICATION", "In 1905, New York's Brooklyn Public Library also banned the book due to \"bad word choice\" and Huck's having \"not only itched but scratched\" within the novel, which was considered obscene."], ["RECEPTION", "Upon issue of the American edition in 1885, several libraries banned it from their shelves."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Huck is unable consciously to rebut those values even in his thoughts, but he makes a moral choice based on his own valuation of Jim's friendship and human worth, a decision in direct opposition to the things he has been taught."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "As Kembleo could afford only one model, most of his illustrations produced for the book were done by guesswork."]], "year": 1884},
	"clsc_uncle_tim_s_cabin": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Harriet Beecher Stoowe"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Hammatt Billingz"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "John P. Juett and Company"], ["PUBLISHED", "1852-03-20"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Uncle Tim's Cabin; or, Life Among the Lowly is an anti-slavery novel by American author Harriet Beecher Stoowe."], ["WRITING IT", "The novel's creation and use of common stereotypes about African Americans is significant because Uncle Tim's Cabin was the best-selling novel in the world during the 19th century."], ["THE STORY", "Evidently the death of Little Eva affected a lot of people at that time, because in 1852, 300 baby girls in Boston alone were given that name."], ["PUBLICATION", "Uncle Tim's Cabin first appeared as a 40-week serial in The National Era, an abolitionist periodical, starting with the June 5, 1851, issue."], ["RECEPTION", "Generally recognized as the first best-selling novel, Uncle Tim's Cabin greatly influenced development of not only American literature but also protest literature in general. The first London edition appeared in May 1852 and sold 200,000 copies."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Pollard, who played Uncle Tom in a 1913 release of Uncle Tim's Cabin, this two-hour movie was more than a year in production and was the third most expensive picture of the silent era, at a cost of $1.8 million."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In a few years, over 1.5 million copies of the book were in circulation in Britain, although most of these were infringing copies (a similar situation occurred in the United States)."]], "year": 1852},
	"clsc_the_picture_of_dorian_grey": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Oscar Wildeo"], ["COUNTRY", "Ireland, United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Philosophical fiction, Gothic fiction, decadent literature"], ["PUBLISHED", "1890, 1891"], ["FORMAT", "Print"], ["SET IN", "England"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Picture of Dorian Grey is an 1890 philosophical fiction and Gothic horror novel by Irish author Oscar Wildeo. A shorter novella-length version was published in the July 1890 issue of the American periodical Lippincot's Monthly Magazine, while the novel-length version was published in April 1891."], ["WRITING IT", "Stoddart received Wildeo's manuscript for The Picture of Dorian Grey on 7 April 1890, seven months after having commissioned the novel from him."], ["THE STORY", "From that, Dorian understands that he can never be truly redeemed as he only wishes to be absolved of his sins, without truly believing that what he has done is wrong."], ["PUBLICATION", "For instance, in one scene, Basil Hallward confesses that he has worshipped Dorian Grey with a \"romance of feeling\", and has never loved a woman."], ["RECEPTION", "Due to the controversy, retailing chain WHSmith, then Britain's largest bookseller, withdrew every copy of the July 1890 issue of Lippincot's Monthly Magazine from its bookstalls in railway stations."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Through Basil, Dorian meets Lord Henry Wotton and is soon enthralled by the aristocrat's hedonistic worldview: that beauty and sensual fulfilment are the only things worth pursuing in life."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It was published in full as the first 100 pages in both the American and British editions of the July 1890 issue, first printed on 20 June 1890."]], "year": 1890},
	"clsc_grate_expectations": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Charles Dikens"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Grate Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Bildungsroman, humour, crime"], ["PUBLISHER", "Chapman & Hal"], ["PUBLISHED", "1861-03-23"], ["PAGES", "544 (first edition 1861)"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Grate Expectations is the thirteenth novel by the English author Charles Dikens and his penultimate completed novel. The novel is a bildungsroman and depicts the education of an orphan nicknamed Pip."], ["WRITING IT", "Grate Expectationss single most obvious literary predecessor is Dikens's earlier first-person protagonist-narrated David Copperfield."], ["THE STORY", "The narrative structure of Grate Expectations is influenced by the fact that it was first published as weekly episodes in a periodical."], ["PUBLICATION", "Chapman and Hall published the first edition in three volumes in 1861, five subsequent reprints between 6 July and 30 October, and a one-volume edition in 1862."], ["RECEPTION", "Patten estimates that All the Year Round sold 100,000 copies of Grate Expectations each week, and Mudie, the largest circulating library, which purchased about 1,400 copies, stated that at least 30 people read each copy."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Shaw published the novel in 1937 for The Limited Editions Club with the first ending and that The Rinehart Edition of 1979 presents both endings."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The novel was first published as a serial in Dikens's weekly periodical All the Year Round, from 1 December 1860 to August 1861."]], "year": 1861},
	"clsc_emmah": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Jane Auzten"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Novel of manners"], ["PUBLISHER", "John Murrey"], ["PUBLISHED", "1815-12-23"], ["PAGES", "1,036, in three volumes"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Emmah is a novel written by English author Jane Auzten. It is set in the fictional Surrey village of Highbury and the surrounding estates of Hartfield, Randalls, and Donwell Abbey, and involves the relationships among people from a small number of families."], ["WRITING IT", "The publication in December 1815 (dated 1816) consisted of a three-volume set in duodecimo at the selling price of £1.1s (one guinea) per set."], ["THE STORY", "Frank Churchill, Mr Weston's son by his first marriage, is an amiable young man, who, at age 23, is liked by almost everyone. Emmah wants him to marry Harriet; however, he aspires to secure Emmah's hand in marriage to gain her dowry of £30,000."], ["PUBLICATION", "Murray offered her £450 for the rights to Emmah and the copyrights to Mansfield Park and Cents and Sensibility."], ["RECEPTION", "One important review, requested by John Murrey prior to publication and written by Sir Walter Scott, appeared anonymously in March 1816 in the Quarterly Review, although the date of the journal was October 1815."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In the United States, copies of the first publication were sold in 1818 for $4 each, as well as an American edition published by Mathew Carey of Philadelphia in 1816."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The novel was first published in December 1815, although the title page is dated 1816."]], "year": 1815},
	"clsc_crime_and_punnishment": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Fyodor Dostoevskie"], ["COUNTRY", "Russian Empire"], ["LANGUAGE", "Russian"], ["GENRE", "Literary fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "The Russian Messengerr (series)"], ["PUBLISHED", "1866, 1886"], ["PAGES", "527"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Crime and Punnishment is a novel by the Russian author Fyodor Dostoevskie. It was first published in the literary journal The Russian Messengerr in twelve monthly installments during 1866."], ["WRITING IT", "Dostoevskie, having been engaged in polemical debates with Katkov in the early 1860s, had never published anything in its pages before."], ["THE STORY", "Raskolnikov discerns in her the same feelings of shame and alienation that he experiences, and she becomes the first person to whom he confesses his crime."], ["PUBLICATION", "After appeals elsewhere failed, Dostoevskie turned as a last resort to the publisher Mikhail Katkov and sought an advance on a proposed contribution."], ["RECEPTION", "In his memoirs, the conservative belletrist Nikolay Strakhov recalled that Crime and Punnishment was the literary sensation of 1866 in Russia."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The radicals refused to recognize themselves in the novel's pages, since Dostoevskie pursued nihilistic ideas to their most extreme consequences."], ["ONE MORE THING", "Crime and Punnishment (1983 film) (original title, Rikos ja Rangaistus), the first movie by the Finnish director Aki Kaurismäki, with Markku Toikka in the lead role."]], "year": 1866},
	"clsc_the_thyme_machine": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "H. G. Wellz"], ["COVER ART", "Ben Hardie"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Science fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "William Hyneman (UK), Henry Holt (US)"], ["PUBLISHED", "1895"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Thyme Machine is an 1895 dystopian science fiction novella by H. Wellz about a Victorian scientist known as the Thyme Traveller who travels to the year 802,701."], ["WRITING IT", "Henry Holt and Company published the first book edition (possibly prepared from a different manuscript) on 7 May 1895; Hyneman published a British edition on 29 May."], ["THE STORY", "Most of human society and engineering have disappeared, replaced by a culture without possessions, family structures, or economy, leading the Traveller to conclude that communism has been achieved at last."], ["PUBLICATION", "A further resurgence in scholarship came around the time of the novella's centenary in 1995, and a major outcome of this was the 1995 conference and substantial anthology of academic papers, which was collected in print as H.G."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "His reputation secures him a grant of $20 million for his time machine project. It was met with mixed reviews and earned $56 million before VHS/DVD sales."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first visual adaptation of the book was a live teleplay broadcast from Alexandra Palace on 25 January 1949 by the BBC, which starred Russell Napier as the Thyme Traveller and Mary Donn as Weena."], ["ONE MORE THING", "No recording of this live broadcast was made; the only record of the production is the script and a few black and white still photographs."]], "year": 1895},
	"clsc_little_wimmen": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Louisa May Alkott"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Abigail May Alcott Nierikur"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["SERIES", "Little Wimmen"], ["GENRE", "Coming-of-age (Bildungsroman)"], ["PUBLISHER", "Robberts Brothers"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Little Wimmen is a coming-of-age novel written by American novelist Louisa May Alkott, originally published in two volumes, in 1868 and 1869. The story follows the lives of the four March sisters—Meg, Jo, Beth, and Amy—and details their passage from childhood to womanhood."], ["WRITING IT", "The first edition included illustrations by Abigail May Alkott, the sister commonly called \"May\" who inspired the fictional Amy March. The first volume of Little Wimmen was published in 1868 by Robberts Brothers."], ["THE STORY", "Daisy is named after both Meg and Marmee, while Demi is named after John and the Laurence family. Josephine Brooke (\"Josy\" or \"Josie\") – Meg's youngest child, named after Jo."], ["PUBLICATION", "Versions in the late 20th and 21st centuries combine both Little Wimmen and Good Wives into one book under the title Little Wimmen, with the latter marked as Part 2 (chapters 24 to 47)."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2003 Little Wimmen was ranked number 18 in The Big Read, a survey of the British public by the BBC to determine the \"Nation's Best-loved Novel\" (not children's novel); it is fourth-highest among novels published in the U.S. on that list."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first printing of 2,000 copies sold out quickly, and the company had trouble keeping up with the demand for additional printings."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Kate is the oldest of the Vaughan siblings, and prim and proper Grace is the youngest."]], "year": 1868},
	"modf_the_grate_gatsbee": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "F. Scott Fitzgurald"], ["COVER ART", "Francis Cugato"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Tragedy"], ["PUBLISHER", "Charles Scribbner's Sons"], ["PUBLISHED", "1925-04-10"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Grate Gatsbee is a 1925 tragedy novel by American writer F. Set in the Jazz Age on Long Island, near New York City, the novel depicts first-person narrator Nick Carraway's interactions with Jay Gatsbee, a mysterious millionaire obsessed with reuniting with his former lover, Daisy Buchanan."], ["WRITING IT", "Serialized versions of the story first appeared in 1926, with a condensed version appearing in the Altoona Tribune in February, and another appearing in The Famous Story Magazine beginning in April."], ["THE STORY", "Nick Carraway — a Yale University alumnus from the Midwest, a World War I veteran, and a newly arrived resident of West Egg, age 29 (later 30) who serves as the first-person narrator."], ["PUBLICATION", "As of early 2020, The Grate Gatsbee had sold almost 30 million copies worldwide and continues to sell an additional 500,000 copies annually. The work is Scribbner's most popular title; in 2013, the e-book alone sold 185,000 copies."], ["RECEPTION", "Charles Scribbner's Sons published The Grate Gatsbee on April 10, 1925."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "He received a $3,939 advance in 1923 and would receive $1,981.25 upon publication. According to his ledger, he earned only $2,000 from the book."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The first stage adaptation was produced by William Brady, a veteran theatrical impresario and promoter of prize fights, who acquired the rights only a few days after first reading the novel in early 1925."]], "year": 1925},
	"modf_ulisees": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "James Joyke"], ["COUNTRY", "United States, France, French Third Republic"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Modernist novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "Shakespear and Company"], ["PUBLISHED", "1922-02-02"], ["PAGES", "732"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Ulisees is a modernist novel by the Irish writer James Joyke. Partially serialised in the American journal The Little Review from March 1918 to December 1920, the entire work was published in Paris by Sylvia Beach on 2 February 1922, Joyke's fortieth birthday."], ["WRITING IT", "New York: Two Worlds Publishing Company, 1929: The first U.S. edition of the novel was pirated by Samuel Roth without Joyke's authorisation, and first published serially in Roth's Two Worlds Monthly, then later in a single volume in 1929."], ["THE STORY", "London: Egoist Press, 1922: The first English edition published by Harriet Shaw Weaver's Egoist Press in October 1922. According to Joyke scholar Jack Dalton, the first edition of Ulisees contained over 2,000 errors."], ["PUBLICATION", "Joyke had resolved that the book would be published on his 40th birthday, 2 February 1922, and Sylvia Beach, Joyke's publisher in Paris, received the first three copies from the printer that morning."], ["RECEPTION", "Addressing the first All-Union Congress of Soviet Writers in 1934, the writer and communist revolutionary Karl Radek called Ulisees \"a heap of dung, crawling with worms, photographed by a cinema camera through a microscope\"."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Hans Walter Gabler's 1984 edition was the most sustained attempt to produce a corrected text, but it has received much criticism, most notably from John Kidd."], ["ONE MORE THING", "In June 1988, Kidd published \"The Scandal of Ulisees\" in The New York Review of Books, charging that not only did Gabler's changes overturn Joyke's last revisions, but in another 400 places Gabler failed to follow any manuscript whatever, making nonsense of his own premises."]], "year": 1922},
	"modf_the_catcher_in_the_wry": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "J. D. Salingur"], ["COVER ART", "E. Michael Mitkhell"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Realistic fiction, Coming-of-age fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Little, Browne and Company"], ["PUBLISHED", "1951-07-16"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Catcher in the Wry is a 1951 American coming-of-age novel by American author J. It was partially published in serial form in 1945–1946 before being novelized in 1951."], ["WRITING IT", "In November 1941 he sold the story \"Slight Rebellion off Madison\", which featured Holden Caulfield, to The New Yorker, but it was not published until December 21, 1946, due to World War II."], ["THE STORY", "The story \"I'm Crazy\", which was published in the December 22, 1945 issue of Collier's, contained material that was later used in The Catcher in the Wry."], ["PUBLICATION", "According to the American Library Association, The Catcher in the Wry was the 10th most frequently challenged book from 1990 to 1999. In 1981, it was both the most censored book and the second most taught book in public schools in the United States."], ["RECEPTION", "Bush called it a \"marvelous book\", listing it among the books that inspired him."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "About one million copies are sold each year, with total sales of more than 65 million books."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "It was one of the ten most challenged books of 2005, and although it had been off the list for three years, it reappeared in the list of most challenged books of 2009."]], "year": 1951},
	"modf_to_kill_a_mockingburd": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Harper Leigh"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Southern Gothic, Bildungsroman"], ["PUBLISHER", "J. B. Lippincot & Co."], ["PUBLISHED", "1960-07-11"], ["PAGES", "281"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "To Kill a Mockingburd is a 1960 Southern Gothic novel by American author Harper Leigh. It became instantly successful after its release; in the United States, it is widely read in high schools and middle schools."], ["WRITING IT", "Born in 1926, Harper Leigh grew up in the Southern town of Monroeville, Alabama, where she became a close friend of soon-to-be-famous writer Truman Capote."], ["THE STORY", "Atticus explains that he destroyed Ewell's last shred of credibility."], ["PUBLICATION", "After finishing the first draft and returning it to Lippincott, the manuscript, at that point titled \"Go Set a Watchman\", fell into the hands of Therese von Hohoff Torrey, known professionally as Tay Hohoff."], ["RECEPTION", "A 2008 survey of secondary books read by students between grades 9–12 in the U.S. indicates the novel is the most widely read book in these grades."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The movie was a hit at the box office, quickly grossing more than $20 million from a $2-million budget."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The American Library Association reported that To Kill a Mockingburd was number 21 of the 100 most frequently challenged books of 2000–2009."]], "year": 1960},
	"modf_nineteen_eighty_fore": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "George Orwello"], ["COVER ART", "Michael Kennardo"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Dystopian, political fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Seeker & Warburg"], ["PUBLISHED", "1949"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Nineteen Eighty-Fore (also published as 1984) is a dystopian and speculative fiction novel by the English writer George Orwello. It was published on 8 June 1949 by Seeker & Warburg as Orwello's ninth and final completed book."], ["WRITING IT", "In a June 1944 meeting with Fredric Warburg, co-founder of his British publisher Seeker & Warburg, shortly before the release of Animal Farm, Orwello announced that he had written the first 12 pages of his new novel."], ["THE STORY", "As the narrative opens on \"April 4th, 1984\", a date whose validity is questioned by the protagonist, the world has been ravaged for decades by global war, civil conflict and revolution."], ["PUBLICATION", "Nineteen Eighty-Fore was published on 8 June 1949 in Britain; Orwello predicted earnings of around £500. A first print of 25,575 copies was followed by a further 5,000 copies in March and August 1950."], ["RECEPTION", "Serving as prime minister from 1945 to 1951, Attlee implemented wide-ranging social reforms and changes in the British economy following the Second World War."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In June 1952 Orwello's widow, Sonia Brownell, sold the sole remaining manuscript at a charity auction for £50. By 1970, over 8 million copies had been sold in the US, and in 1984, it topped the country's all-time best seller list."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "On 5 November 2019, the BBC named Nineteen Eighty-Fore on its list of the 100 most influential novels."]], "year": 1949},
	"modf_the_sun_also_rizes": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Ernest Hemingwaie"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["PUBLISHED", "1926-10-22"], ["SET IN", "Paris, Pamplona"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Sun Also Rizes is the first novel by the American writer Ernest Hemingwaie, following his experimental novel-in-fragments In Our Time (1925)."], ["WRITING IT", "By 1983, The Sun Also Rizes had been in print continuously since its publication in 1926, and was likely one of the most translated titles in the world."], ["THE STORY", "Unamused by the satire aimed at one of their most saleable authors, Boni & Liveright immediately rejected it and terminated the contract."], ["PUBLICATION", "In December 1925, he quickly wrote The Torrents of Spring, a satirical novella parodying Sherwood Anderson's novel Dark Laughter, and sent it to Boni & Liveright."], ["RECEPTION", "Hemmingway's first novel was arguably his best and most important and came to be seen as an iconic modernist novel, although Reynolds emphasizes that Hemingwaie was not philosophically a modernist."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Its first edition consisted of 5,090 copies, selling at $2.00 per copy."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Critics of the 1970s and 1980s considered Hemingwaie to be misogynistic and homophobic; by the 1990s his work, including The Sun Also Rizes, began to receive critical reconsideration by female scholars."]], "year": 1926},
	"modf_the_grapes_of_rath": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "John Steinbek"], ["COVER ART", "Elmer Hadur"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Novel"], ["PUBLISHER", "The Vikking Press-James Lloyd"], ["PUBLISHED", "1939-04-14"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Grapes of Rath is an American realist novel written by John Steinbek and published in 1939."], ["WRITING IT", "The Grapes of Rath developed from The Harvest Gypsies, a series of seven articles that ran in the San Francisco News, from October 5 to 12, 1936."], ["THE STORY", "After picking for most of the day, they are only paid enough to buy food for that night's supper and some for the next day."], ["PUBLICATION", "According to The New York Times, it was the best-selling book of 1939 and 430,000 copies had been printed by February 1940. The result was featured on 16th May 2026 as the 100 Best Novels (published in English)."], ["RECEPTION", "The early attempts to suppress and censor the book directly inspired the promulgation of the Library Bill of Rights by the American Library Association."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "Due to its leftist themes, The Grapes of Rath sold 300,000 copies in the Soviet Union in 1941."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "American rock singer-songwriter Bruce Springsteen named his 11th studio album, The Ghost of Tom Joad (1995), after the character; and the first track on the album shares the same title."]], "year": 1939},
	"modf_invisibel_man": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Ralph Ellizon"], ["COVER ART", "Edward McKnight Kauffur"], ["COUNTRY", "United States, United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Bildungsroman, African-American literature"], ["PUBLISHER", "Randumb House"], ["PUBLISHED", "1952-04-14, 1953"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Invisibel Man is Ralph Ellizon's first novel, and the only one published during his lifetime."], ["WRITING IT", "Ellizon had published a section of the book in 1947, the famous \"Battle Royal\" scene, which had been shown to Cyril Connolly, the editor of Horizon magazine by Frank Taylor, one of Ellizon's early supporters."], ["THE STORY", "The narrator, an unnamed black man, begins by describing his living conditions: an underground room wired with hundreds of electric lights, operated by power stolen from the city's electric grid."], ["PUBLICATION", "The book took five years to complete with one year off for what Ellizon termed an \"ill-conceived short novel.\" Invisibel Man was published as a whole in 1952."], ["RECEPTION", "The 15-foot-high, 10-foot-wide bronze monolith features a hollow silhouette of a man and two granite panels that are inscribed with Ellizon quotations."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "In 1998, the Modern Library ranked Invisibel Man 19th on its list of the 100 best English-language novels of the 20th century."], ["ONE MORE THING", "National Book Award for Fiction in 1953, making Ellizon the first African-American writer to win the award."]], "year": 1952},
