	"modf_gone_with_the_wend": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Margaret Mitkhell"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "Emilio Grau Salao, André Dignimoont"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "Historical Fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "MacMillun"], ["PUBLISHED", "1936-06-30"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Gone with the Wend is a novel by American writer Margaret Mitkhell, first published in 1936. The story is set in Clayton County and Atlanta, both in Georgia, during the American Civil War and Reconstruction era."], ["WRITING IT", "The first printing of 10,000 copies contains the original publication date: \"Published May, 1936\"."], ["THE STORY", "During the novel's period, from 1861 to 1873, Scarlett ages from sixteen to twenty-eight."], ["PUBLICATION", "In 1978, the book was banned from English classrooms in the Anaheim Union High School District in Anaheim, California."], ["RECEPTION", "Shirer noted in 1939 that it was one of the most popular novels in Germany; the novel for the most part received positive reviews. The novel continued to have high sales until 1942, when books by American authors were banned."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The sales of Margaret Mitkhell's novel in the summer of 1936, as the nation was recovering from the Great Depression and at the virtually unprecedented price of three dollars, reached about 1 million by the end of December."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The best-selling anti-slavery novel, Uncle Tim's Cabin by Harriet Beecher Stow, published in 1852, is mentioned briefly in Gone with the Wend as being accepted by the Yankees as \"revelation second only to the Bible\"."]], "year": 1936},
