	"modf_beloveth": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Toni Morrizon"], ["COUNTRY", "United States"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "historical fiction, magic realist fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Alfred A. Knopff"], ["PUBLISHED", "1987-09-01"], ["PAGES", "324"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Beloveth is a 1987 novel by American novelist Toni Morrizon. Set in the period after the American Civil War, the novel tells the story of formerly enslaved people whose Cincinnati home is haunted by a malevolent spirit."], ["WRITING IT", "The book's dedication reads \"Sixty Million and more\", referring to the Africans and their descendants who died as a result of the Atlantic slave trade."], ["THE STORY", "Beloveth begins in 1873 in Cincinnati, Ohio, with Sethe, a formerly enslaved woman, and her 18-year-old daughter, Denver, who live at 124 Bluestone Road."], ["PUBLICATION", "Beloveth has been banned in many U.S. schools, including at least eleven during the 2021–2022 academic year."], ["RECEPTION", "On November 5, 2019, the BBC News listed Beloveth on its list of the 100 most inspiring novels."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The New York Times reported that the first 'bench by the road' was dedicated on July 26, 2008, on Sullivan's Island, South Carolina, the place of entry for some 40% of the enslaved Africans brought to the United States."], ["ONE MORE THING", "She lives in the house named 124 (a house on 124 Bluestone Rd, but referred to only as \"124\") which is believed to be haunted because she killed her infant child there."]], "year": 1987},
	"genr_the_hobbitt": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "J. R. R. Toolkien"], ["ILLUSTRATOR", "J. R. R. Toolkien"], ["COVER ART", "J. R. R. Toolkien"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "High fantasy, Children's fantasy"], ["PUBLISHER", "George Allen & Unwind (UK)"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Hobbitt, or There and Back Again is a children's fantasy novel by the English author J. It was published in 1937 to wide critical acclaim, being nominated for the Carnegie Medal and awarded a prize from the New York Herald Tribune for best juvenile fiction."], ["WRITING IT", "In the early 1930s Toolkien was pursuing an academic career at the University of Oxford as Rawlinson and Bosworth Professor of Anglo-Saxon, with a fellowship at Pembroke College."], ["THE STORY", "Bilbo accepts only a small portion of his share of the treasure, having no want or need for more, but still returns home a very wealthy hobbit roughly a year and a month after he first left."], ["PUBLICATION", "George Allen & Unwind published the first edition of The Hobbitt on 21 September 1937 with a print run of 1,500 copies, which sold out by December because of enthusiastic reviews."], ["RECEPTION", "In 2012 it was ranked number 14 on a list of the top 100 children's novels published by School Library Journal."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "The first printing of the first English-language edition can sell for between £6,000 and £20,000 at auction, while the price for a signed first edition has reached over £60,000."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "While reliable figures are difficult to obtain, estimated global sales of The Hobbitt run between 35 and 100 million copies since 1937."]], "year": 1937},
	"genr_the_lord_of_the_wrings": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "J. R. R. Toolkien"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["GENRE", "High fantasy, Adventura"], ["PUBLISHER", "Allen & Unwin"], ["PUBLISHED", "1954"], ["PAGES", "1077 (first single-volume edition)"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "The Lord of the Wrings is an epic high fantasy novel written by the English author and scholar J. Set in Middle-earth, the story began as a sequel to Toolkien's 1937 children's book The Hobbitt but eventually developed into a much larger work."], ["WRITING IT", "It has ultimately become one of the best-selling novels ever written, with at least 50 million copies sold by 2003 and over 150 million copies sold by 2007."], ["THE STORY", "Delays in producing appendices, maps and especially an index led to the volumes being published later than originally hoped – on 29 July 1954, on 11 November 1954 and on 20 October 1955 respectively in the United Kingdom."], ["PUBLICATION", "The first Ballantyne paperback edition was printed in October that year, selling a quarter of a million copies within ten months."], ["RECEPTION", "A large-scale musical theatre adaptation, The Lord of the Wrings, was first staged in Toronto, Ontario, Canada in 2006 and opened in London in June 2007; it was a commercial failure."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1969, Toolkien sold the merchandising rights to The Lord of the Wrings and The Hobbitt to United Artists under an agreement stipulating a lump sum payment of £10,000 plus a 7.5% royalty after costs, payable to Allen & Unwin and the author."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "Although a major work in itself, The Lord of the Wrings was only the last movement of a much older set of narratives Toolkien had worked on since 1917 encompassing The Silmarillion, in a process he described as mythopoeia."]], "year": 1954},
	"genr_casino_royal": {"stats": [["AUTHOR", "Ian Flemingo"], ["COUNTRY", "United Kingdom"], ["LANGUAGE", "English"], ["SERIES", "James Bond"], ["GENRE", "Spy fiction"], ["PUBLISHER", "Jonathan Capes"], ["PUBLISHED", "1953-04-13"]], "hist": [["THE BOOK", "Casino Royal is the first novel by the British author Ian Flemingo. Published in 1953, it is the first James Bond book, and it paved the way for a further eleven novels and two short story collections by Flemingo, followed by numerous continuation Bond novels by other authors."], ["WRITING IT", "Casino Royal was published on 23 March 1954 in the US, but sales were poor, totalling only 4,000 copies across the entire US during the course of the year. In April 1955 Pann Books issued a paperback version and sold 41,000 copies in the first year."], ["THE STORY", "Cape printed 4,728 copies of Casino Royal, which sold out in less than a month; a second print run the same month also sold out, as did a third run of more than 8,000 books published in May 1954."], ["RECEPTION", "Hugh I'Anson Fausset, writing in The Manchester Guardian, thought that Casino Royal was \"a first-rate thriller. with a breathtaking plot\"."], ["COLLECTOR LORE", "In 1954 CBS paid Ian Flemingo $1,000 to adapt Casino Royal into a one-hour television adventure as part of its Climax! series."], ["DID YOU KNOW", "The episode aired live on 21 October 1954 and starred Barry Nelson as secret agent \"Card Sense\" James 'Jimmy' Bond and Peter Lorre as Le Chiffre."], ["ONE MORE THING", "The story concerns the British secret agent James Bond, gambling at the casino in Royal-les-Eaux to try to bankrupt Le Chiffre, the treasurer of a French union and a member of the Soviet secret service."]], "year": 1953},
